  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, let's tackle this forecast question. So the user wants to know the probability that by certain dates, one of the Millennium Prize Problems will have a correct solution that includes AI's contribution. The deadlines are 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, 2050.\n\nFirst, I need to recall all the Millennium Prize Problems except the Poincar\u00e9 Conjecture, which was already solved. The remaining are Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer, Hodge, Navier-Stokes, P vs NP, Riemann, and Yang-Mills. Solving any of these requires a deep understanding and significant breakthroughs. \n\nNow, the role of AI here. AI is already assisting in mathematical research, like in automated theorem proving or pattern recognition in complex data. But these problems are particularly tough. For example, P vs NP \u2013 people have been trying for decades without a proof. Would AI contribute a new step? Maybe, but it's still a big leap.\n\nThe key here is that the solution must incorporate a new mathematical argument or proof step from AI. So AI can't just verify a proof that exists without it; it must have a part in creating the proof's new elements. This is more than just computational brute-force. \n\nConsidering the timelines: By 2026, the horizon is tight. It's only a year and a half from the forecast date. The problems are notoriously resistant to solutions. Maybe 5%? Very low. \n\nBy 2030, that's five years. AI is advancing, but solving one of these problems in five years still seems unlikely. Perhaps bumping it to 10-15%.\n\n2035: Ten years from the forecast. More time for tech advancements. Maybe AI becomes better at generating proofs. Maybe some progress. Say 20%?\n\n2040: Fifteen years. More years for AI to evolve, maybe with quantum computing or other advancements. Maybe the probability increases to 30-35%.\n\n2050: Twenty-five years. That's a long time. AI tools could be way more sophisticated, perhaps leading to collaborative breakthroughs. Maybe 50%? But honestly, even after that time, math problems are hard. Maybe 40%? Still, it's all speculative.\n\nI need to ensure the probabilities are non-decreasing. So each subsequent date must have a higher or equal probability than the previous. Let me check my estimates: 2026 (0.05), 2030 (0.1), 2035 (0.2), 2040 (0.35), 2050 (0.4). That's non-decreasing. \n\nWait, but 2050 might be higher. If I think it's 0.4 by 2040 and 0.4 by 2050, that's okay. I can adjust if needed. Let me see. Also, the event might never happen, so the probabilities shouldn't go to 100%. \n\nFinalizing, I'll assign: 2026 - 0.05, 2030 - 0.1, 2035 - 0.2, 2040 - 0.25, 2050 - 0.35. Wait, that was my initial thought. But maybe the 2040 and 2050 can be a bit more. Alternatively, maybe 2040 is 0.25, 2050 to 0.4. Let me stick with that. So final probabilities would be 0.05 (2026), 0.1 (2030), 0.15 (2035), 0.25 (2040), 0.4 (2050). But need to ensure that they're non-decreasing and each step up. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the probabilities increase more gradually. Maybe:\n\n2026: 0.05\n\n2030: 0.08\n\n2035: 0.15\n\n2040: 0.25\n\n2050: 0.4\n\nThat would be non-decreasing. But I need to make sure the reasoning aligns with each date. \n\nConsidering the inherent uncertainty in solving such difficult problems and the role of AI, which is still somewhat experimental in deep mathematical proof generation, the probabilities are more likely to be low but increasing over longer timelines. I think the final probabilities should reflect that. So maybe:\n\n2026: 0.05\n\n2030: 0.1\n\n2035: 0.15\n\n2040: 0.25\n\n2050: 0.4\n\nEach date has a higher probability, and it's non-decreasing. The probabilities don't reach 1, accounting for the possibility of the event never occurring.\n\nFinal step is to present this as a JSON object without explanations.\n</think>\n\n{\"2026\": 0.05, \"2030\": 0.1, \"2035\": 0.15, \"2040\": 0.25, \"2050\": 0.4}",
